## 🎮 Part 2: Extracting JavaScript Variables from Live WebSockets
Standard web application crawlers are blind to WebSockets (wss://). Because casino engines communicate through persistent, bidirectional socket streams, vulnerabilities hide in the raw binary/JSON variables transmitted when betting.
You can extract these streams directly via the command line using tcpdump and tshark (the terminal version of Wireshark) on your Latitude 3380.
## Step 1: Capture the Raw Traffic
While you are logged into your test account and playing a casino game, capture the raw network packets hitting your network interface.

   1. Find your active network interface (e.g., wlan0 or eth0):
   
   ip link show
   
   2. Sniff the live HTTPS/WSS traffic stream and dump it to a capture file:
   
   sudo tcpdump -i wlan0 port 443 -w casino_traffic.pcap
   
   (Let it run for 1–2 minutes while placing low-stakes bets or navigating game rooms, then press Ctrl + C)

## Step 2: Extract & Decode WebSocket Text Frames
WebSockets masks data payloads. tshark can strip the TLS and masking layers to output the raw, hidden JavaScript arrays and backend variables passing back and forth.
Run this analysis command on your saved file:

tshark -r casino_traffic.pcap -Y "websocket.payload.text" -T fields -e websocket.payload.text > extracted_frames.txt

## Step 3: Hunt for Logic & Variable Assumptions
Open extracted_frames.txt. You will see the raw inner messages the application's engine reads. To find what scripts miss, look for specific string structures:

* Look for Hidden Status Switches:

grep -E "(status|win|win_amount|multiplier|bet)" extracted_frames.txt

* Analyze Object Properties: Look for objects containing key parameters that you can replay or modify via your proxy (e.g., {"event":"bet","amount":10,"currency":"USD","game_id":8420}).

If a third-party framework flagged by your Retire.js automation script is processing these socket frames insecurely (like processing raw strings directly via an old eval() or unvetted parsing library inside the runtime bundle), you have a direct path to trigger high-severity behavior.
